## Sub-subtypes

The Reference type has two sub-subtypes refining it:

- **`resource`** — concrete external artifact (URL, file, document, primary source)
- **`content-type`** — content-typed external artifact (carries content-type metadata for proper rendering / dispatch)

## Canonical body structure

```markdown
## What the facility is

<One paragraph.  Cite the source.>

## When to reach for it

<The trigger conditions.>

## How to use it

<Minimal usage example or shape summary.>

## Project-specific variations

<When different projects vary the convention, note them.>

## See also

<Related references, patterns, anti-patterns.>
```

## Pitfalls

- **Re-implementing the source** — a reference that duplicates the authoritative documentation rots when the source changes; keep references thin and link out
- **Stale URLs** — especially for project-local dashboards or internal wiki pages.  Flag for periodic validation
- **Over-abstraction** — references should name concrete tools, not generic categories.  *"Diagnostic tools"* is not a reference; *"xref + scoped-logging + scope-capture"* is

## Composition with adjacent classes

- **`library`** — when a reference grows into a full study with file:line citations
- **`resource`** — sub-subtype for concrete external artifacts (URLs, primary sources)
- **`content-type`** — sub-subtype for content-typed artifacts
- **`pattern`** — patterns may cite references to ground themselves in external facilities
- **`protocol`** — protocols often cite references for the tools they govern
- **`briefing`** — references are retrieval-oriented; briefings are audience-oriented

